>> title: Italian Chopped Salad
>> category: Salads
>> tags: salami, provolone, chickpeas, romaine, oregano
>> counters: The Bowl Shop
>> aka: chopped salad, italian chop, antipasto salad, la scala chopped salad, the goop father, italian sub salad, chopped italian
>> pairs-with: basic-vinaigrette
>> servings: 4
>> time: 45 min
>> step.2: crisp 425°F 25 min
>> step.3: macerate 20 min
>> step.4: chop to one size
>> step.5: whisk in
>> step.6: toss

Preheat the #oven{} to 425°F (220°C).

Crisp @chickpeas{1%can}(15 oz; drained, rinsed, dried hard in a towel) with @extra-virgin olive oil{2%Tbs}(30 mL), @dried oregano{1%tsp} and @kosher salt{1/2%tsp} on a #sheet pan{} for ~roast{25%min}, shaking the pan twice. Dry is the whole job — a wet chickpea steams itself soft in its own skin no matter how hot the oven is, and the towel is doing more work than the heat.

Macerate @red onion{1/2%small}(sliced paper thin) in @red wine vinegar{3%Tbs}(45 mL) for ~stand{20%min}. The onion comes out pink and mild and the vinegar comes out oniony, and both halves of that go into the dressing.

Chop @romaine{1%head}, @radicchio{1/2%head}, @salami{4%oz}(115 g), @provolone{4%oz}(115 g), @cherry tomatoes{1%cup}(halved) and @pepperoncini{1/4%cup}(stemmed) so that every piece is the same half-inch. This is not a way of preparing the ingredients, it is the recipe: the reason a chopped salad is worth ordering is that one forkful holds all six things, and the only thing making that true is that they are all cut to the size of a fork.

Whisk @&(~2)macerated onion{} and its vinegar with @extra-virgin olive oil{1/3%cup}(80 mL), @dried oregano{1%tsp} and @black pepper{1/2%tsp}.

Toss @&(~2)chopped salad{} with @&(~1)dressing{} and @&(~4)crisp chickpeas{}, and eat it inside ten minutes.

Dress it at the table. The tomatoes weep the moment the dressing lands and the chickpeas soften from the outside.
